.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1229 PM PST Fri Nov 6 2026
(This evening through Saturday night)

High pressure building in from the Eastern Pacific continues to
dominate our weather pattern across the Bay Area and north Central
Coast. As such, afternoon temperatures are forecast to be some
2-11 deg F above normal through the remainder of the upcoming
weekend. Morning minimum temperatures will be similarly warm.
Thus, afternoon maximum forecast temperatures will be in the mid-
to-upper 60s with interior valleys reaching the low-to-mid 70s.
The greatest potential for reaching 80 deg F will be across the
interior Central Coast.

Please see the BEACHES section of the AFD below for more on the
Beach Hazards Statement and Coastal Flood Advisory.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1229 PM PST Fri Nov 6 2026
(Sunday through next Thursday)

On Sunday, a weakening frontal boundary is forecast to move into
Northern California and brush the North Bay Sunday afternoon and
evening. However, as the boundary continues to weaken, little to no
rainfall is expected. That said, a shallow marine layer is forecast
to return. A slight cool down is expected across the North Bay
Sunday and Monday, however temperatures still will remain above
seasonal averages. Either weak ridging or zonal flow aloft will
maintain dry weather conditions and warm temperatures through at
least Wednesday.

Thursday and beyond, expecting the mid/upper level trough to deepen
along or off of the West Coast. This will result in the potential
for unsettled weather conditions to return to the region. The
Climate Prediction Center`s 6 to 10 Day Outlook (12th - 16th) has
a 65% to 80% probability of greater than normal precipitation.
It`s still to far out in time given the model uncertainty late
next week and beyond, yet be prepared for rain to return to the
region!

