.BEACHES...
Issued at 1229 PM PST Fri Nov 6 2026

Beach Hazards Statement:

Hazardous beach conditions will be present at Pacific Coast
beaches through Monday as long period (14-18 second)
westerly/northwesterly swell will bring an increased risk for
sneaker waves and strong rip currents. Breaking waves of 12 to 16
feet are expected at exposed beaches. Spring tides will also
increase the currents and bring higher tides than normal. Waves
can sweep across the shoreline without warning, pulling people
into the sea from beaches, jetties, and rocks. Stay off of
jetties, piers, rocks, and other waterside infrastructure. Remain
out of the water to avoid hazardous surf and never turn your back
on the ocean!

Coastal Flood Advisory:

A combination of high astronomical tides and thermal expansion
from warmer than normal water will result in tidal flooding
concerns across Bayshore locations in the San Francisco, San
Pablo, and Monterey Bays. At the San Francisco tide gauge, high
tide is expected 1.6 ft above normal (7.4 ft MLLW) at 9:38 AM
Saturday 11/7, 1.6 ft above normal (7.4 ft MLLW) at 10:06 AM
Sunday 11/8, 1.6 ft above normal (7.4 ft MLLW) at 10:35 AM Monday
11/9, 1.5 ft above normal (7.3 ft MLLW) at 11:04 AM Tuesday 11/10,
1.4 ft above normal (7.2 ft MLLW) at 11:35 AM Wednesday 11/11,
1.4 ft above normal (7.2 ft MLLW) at 12:10 PM Thursday 11/12, 1.2
ft above normal (7.0 ft MLLW) at 12:49 PM Friday 11/13. These
predictions include up to 1.5 ft of surge that will enhance the
astronomical tide and flooding threat. High tide varies up to 90
minutes earlier or later along the Pacific Coast and through the
San Francisco Bay, respectively. In addition, long-period swell
will create hazardous conditions along the Pacific coast beaches
with an increased risk of sneaker waves and rip currents.

These Spring Tides are due to a new moon on Monday, which is much
lower in the sky this time of year compared to a full moon. In 2
weeks, the astronomical tides will be about 0.8` higher than this
round, and the total water level could be more than a foot higher
if we get SW winds and/or lower pressure. This combination would
bring the total water level near the 1% annual exceedance
probability (2.85` above normal), or a 1 in 100 year event.
Something to keep an eye on for the week of Thanksgiving.

